<misterromdescription>
  <name>Puzzle Bobble 2 (Ver 2.2J 1995/07/20)</name>
  <setname>pbobble2j</setname>
  <year>1995</year>
  <manufacturer>Taito</manufacturer>
  <rbf>TaitoF3</rbf>
  <players>2</players>
  <joystick>8-way</joystick>
  <rotation>horizontal</rotation>
  <region>Japan</region>

  <!-- ============================================================
       UNTESTED, AND EXPECTED TO RENDER WRONG. Read this before
       filing a bug against it.

       This game is extend=0 (512x512 playfields). The renderer is
       HARDWIRED to extend=1 (1024x512), so the tilemap stride is
       wrong for this set and the backgrounds should come out
       scrambled while the sprites look about right. That is the
       expected result, not a defect in this MRA. It is here to
       show exactly what extend=0 costs, because implementing it
       unlocks the most games of any single change: all three
       Puzzle Bobbles plus Darius Gaiden, Cleopatra Fortune, Grid
       Seeker, Space Invaders '95 and Gekirindan.

       WHY THE JAPANESE SET AND NOT THE PARENT: MAME's parent
       pbobble2 (Ver 2.3O) runs init_pbobbl2p, which PATCHES the
       program ROM at 0x40090/0x40094, NOPping a branch its own
       comment calls "protection check?? or some kind of checksum
       fail?" that otherwise crashes the game. No such patch can
       exist here, and real hardware presumably ran the ROM as
       dumped. pbobble2j (Ver 2.2J) uses init_pbobble2, which
       patches nothing, so it is the honest target. If the parent
       is ever wanted, it needs that question answered first.

       Sizes fit the universal map with room to spare; this set has
       NO sprites_hi (EMPTY_SPRITE_HIDATA, so 4bpp sprites like
       Bubble Memories) but DOES have tilemap_hi.

         0x0000000  maincpu    2 MB   4-way byte interleave (1 MB + pad)
         0x0200000  audiocpu 512 KB   16-bit interleave (68000)
         0x0280000  sprites    4 MB   16-bit interleave (2 MB + pad)
         0x0680000  sprites_hi 2 MB   ZEROS - no ROM in this set
         0x0880000  tilemap    4 MB   LOAD32_WORD pair (2 MB + pad)
         0x0C80000  tilemap_hi 2 MB   (1 MB + pad)
         0x0E80000  ensoniq    4 MB   2x 2 MB, plain
         0x1280000             = 18.5 MB total
       ============================================================ -->
  <rom index="0" zip="pbobble2j.zip|pbobble2.zip" md5="None">

    <!-- maincpu: ROM_LOAD32_BYTE x4 -->
    <interleave output="32">
      <part name="e10-11.20" crc="b82f81da" map="0001"/>
      <part name="e10-10.19" crc="f432267a" map="0010"/>
      <part name="e10-09.18" crc="e0b1b599" map="0100"/>
      <part name="e10-08.17" crc="4ccec344" map="1000"/>
    </interleave>

    <!-- pad: maincpu 1 MB -> 2 MB -->
    <part repeat="1048576">00</part>

    <!-- audiocpu 68000: ROM_LOAD16_BYTE pair -->
    <interleave output="16">
      <part name="e10-12.32" crc="b92dc8ad" map="01"/>
      <part name="e10-13.33" crc="87842c13" map="10"/>
    </interleave>

    <!-- sprites: ROM_LOAD16_BYTE pair -->
    <interleave output="16">
      <part name="e10-02.rom" crc="c0564490" map="01"/>
      <part name="e10-01.rom" crc="8c26ff49" map="10"/>
    </interleave>

    <!-- pad: sprites 2 MB -> 4 MB -->
    <part repeat="2097152">00</part>

    <!-- sprites_hi: none in this set (EMPTY_SPRITE_HIDATA) -->
    <part repeat="2097152">00</part>

    <!-- tilemap: ROM_LOAD32_WORD pair -->
    <interleave output="32">
      <part name="e10-07.rom" crc="dcb3c29b" map="0021"/>
      <part name="e10-06.rom" crc="1b0f20e2" map="2100"/>
    </interleave>

    <!-- pad: tilemap 2 MB -> 4 MB -->
    <part repeat="2097152">00</part>

    <!-- tilemap_hi -->
    <part name="e10-05.rom" crc="81266151"/>

    <!-- pad: tilemap_hi 1 MB -> 2 MB -->
    <part repeat="1048576">00</part>

    <!-- ensoniq sample ROMs, plain (MAME 0x000000 and 0x400000) -->
    <part name="e10-04.rom" crc="5c0862a6"/>
    <part name="e10-03.rom" crc="46d68ac8"/>
  </rom>

  <!-- Game config (Rayforce.sv "GAME CONFIG"):
         bit [1:0] = 3  visarea f3 (232 lines from line 24), as EAR
         bit [5], [7:6] = id 5. The game id is {bit 5, bits 7:6} since the
                        field was widened past four games. This set used to
                        sit on id 2, which is Bubble Bobble II's, and would
                        have been judged against that game's checksums the
                        moment those arms came back. It now has its own id
                        and its own title row. No expectations are measured
                        for it yet, so id 5 lands on the case default and the
                        checksum rows REPORT what they find.
         bit [2] = 1 asks for NON-extend (eight 32x32 playfields), which is
                        what this game is. Note the inverted sense against
                        MAME's "extend" naming: every MRA written before this
                        bit went live carries 0 there and every one of those
                        games is extend=1, so 0 has to keep meaning extend=1.
                        0x67 = game id 5, visarea f3, non-extend. -->
  <rom index="1">
    <part>67</part>
  </rom>

  <nvram index="254" size="256"/>

  <buttons names="Shot,-,-,-,-,-,Start,Coin,Service,Pause" default="A,B,R,L,Select,Start" count="2"/>
</misterromdescription>
